course: biology
lesson: 363
title: Sleep and dreams in people
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Sleep is not the body switching off: it is a busy night shift when you grow, mend muscles, fight germs and store what you learned that day.
explain: A night is like a ride on a slow wave: you sink from light sleep into deep sleep, rise into dreaming sleep, and do it all again, about every 90 minutes.
explain: Watch a sleeping little brother: sometimes he lies still as a stone in deep sleep, and sometimes his eyes flick under his eyelids, which means he is in dreaming sleep.
explain: The brain also plays tricks at night: twitches as you drop off, nightmares, sleepwalking and dreams that vanish by breakfast are all normal parts of sleep.
fact: Children of 9 or 10 need about 9 to 12 hours of sleep a night and grown-ups about 7 to 9; too little sleep makes it harder to concentrate and makes people grumpy.
fact: A night is made of about 4 to 6 cycles of about 90 minutes, from light sleep to deep sleep to dreaming sleep; deep sleep comes mostly early in the night and dreaming sleep gets longer towards morning.
fact: Everyone wakes for a moment several times a night but usually forgets it.
fact: REM sleep, short for rapid eye movement, is when most vivid dreams happen, and the brain switches off most muscles so you do not act out your dreams.
fact: Babies spend about half their sleep in REM, grown-ups only about a fifth.
fact: Most dreams are forgotten within minutes of waking because the brain does not store new memories well during dreaming sleep; writing them down at once helps.
fact: Nightmares are common in children and do not predict anything; scary films, stress or illness make them more likely, and imagining a new happy ending while awake often helps.
fact: Sleepwalking happens in deep sleep, not while dreaming; children usually grow out of it, and waking a sleepwalker is not dangerous, just confusing, so it is best to guide them gently back to bed.
fact: Sleep helps the brain store what you learned, and rats that ran a maze replay the same brain pattern while they sleep.
fact: Bright screens and exciting games keep the brain alert and can delay melatonin, the sleepy hormone that rises when it gets dark.
fact: Yawns are catching, even reading about yawning can start one, dogs can catch yawns from their owners, and scientists still argue why we yawn.
fact: The sudden jerk as you fall asleep is a common, harmless muscle twitch, and nobody is sure why it happens.
fact: In 1964 a teenager called Randy Gardner stayed awake about 11 days and began seeing things that were not there; record books stopped accepting attempts because it is dangerous.
exercise: About how much sleep does a child of 9 or 10 need each night? | About 9 to 12 hours | About 4 hours | About 20 hours | About 6 hours
exercise: Which of these happens in the body during sleep? | It grows, mends muscles and fights germs | It stops all of its work completely until the morning comes | It forgets everything you learned | It uses up all its energy running
exercise: About how long is one sleep cycle? | About 90 minutes | About 5 minutes | About 12 hours | About 1 week
exercise: When in the night does most deep sleep come? | Early in the night | Only just before waking | Only at midday | Never in children
exercise: What does REM stand for? | Rapid eye movement | Really empty mind | Resting every muscle | Red eye mark
exercise: What happens to most muscles during REM sleep? | The brain switches them off so you do not act out dreams | They work twice as hard as they do in the daytime, so you wake up tired | They grow longer | They become hot and stiff
exercise: Who spends the biggest share of sleep in REM? | Babies, about half of their sleep | Grown-ups, who spend almost all of their sleep in it | Teenagers, none of it | Grandparents, all night
exercise: Why are most dreams forgotten soon after waking? | The brain does not store new memories well during dreaming sleep | Dreams are far too boring and silly for the brain to bother remembering them | The eyes wipe them away | Breakfast smells push them out
exercise: What can help you remember more of your dreams? | Writing them down the moment you wake | Eating a big piece of cheese just before bed | Sleeping with the light on | Staying awake all night
exercise: What do nightmares tell you about the future? | Nothing, they are the brain playing with fears and the day's events | They show what will happen tomorrow, so that you can get ready for it in time | They warn you about danger next week | They mean you are ill
exercise: In which kind of sleep does sleepwalking happen? | Deep sleep | Dreaming sleep | When you are fully awake | Only during naps
exercise: What is the best thing to do with a sleepwalker? | Guide them gently back to bed | Shout loudly in their ear to wake them up | Leave them locked outside | Throw cold water on them
exercise: What did scientists see in rats that had run a maze? | The rats replayed the same brain pattern while they slept | The rats forgot the whole maze straight away after one night | The rats ran the maze in their sleep with their legs | The rats never slept again
exercise: What is melatonin? | The sleepy hormone that rises when it gets dark | A vitamin that you get from eating lots of raw carrots | The muscle that makes you yawn | A colour in your eyes
exercise: How can a bright screen in bed affect sleep? | It keeps the brain alert and can delay melatonin | It makes you fall asleep at once, as soon as you look at it | It gives you more REM sleep | It has no effect at all
exercise: Which animal can catch yawns from its owner? | The dog | The goldfish | The tortoise | The spider
exercise: What is the sudden jerk you sometimes feel as you fall asleep? | A common, harmless muscle twitch | A sign of a serious illness | Your heart stopping for a moment | Proof that you were really falling
exercise: What did Randy Gardner do in 1964? | Stayed awake about 11 days | Slept for 11 days | Dreamed the same dream 11 times | Sleepwalked for 11 kilometres
test: «I'm 10, I only need like 6 hours, same as my dad, right?» What is true? | No, children of 10 need about 9 to 12 hours | Yes, everyone needs exactly 6 hours, children and grown-ups alike | Yes, children need less sleep than adults | No, children need about 20 hours
test: Why is it so much harder to wake up from deep sleep than from light sleep? | In deep sleep the brain is far less ready to notice the world | Deep sleep only happens with eyes open | In deep sleep you are dreaming so loudly that you cannot hear anything else | Light sleep has no brain activity at all
test: «nan says never wake a sleepwalker, they'll die of shock» What is right? | That is an old story; it is just confusing, so guide them gently back to bed | True, waking a sleepwalker gives them such a shock that it stops their heart | True, they will never sleep again | They should be woken with a loud bang
test: Why might it be dangerous if your muscles worked during a dream? | You could act out the dream and hurt yourself | You would sleep far too deeply and might never wake up again | Your dreams would stop | You would grow too fast
test: «gonna stay up all night revising for the test tmrw» Why is this a poor plan? | Sleep helps the brain store what you learned, so you would remember less | Sleep wipes out everything you learned | Tests are easier when you are tired | Revising late at night makes your eyes stronger, but your hands get too tired to write
test: «had a nightmare about a monster under the bed, does that mean one's there?» What is the best answer? | No, nightmares do not predict anything; they are the brain playing with fears | Yes, dreams show what is really in the room while your eyes are shut, so check under the bed | Yes, but only on Fridays | No, monsters only come in the morning
test: Count them: a boy sleeps 9 hours, and one sleep cycle lasts about 90 minutes. About how many cycles does he have? | 6 | 9 | 3 | 12
test: In a cartoon, Tom sleepwalks along a washing line with his eyes shut, dreaming of chasing Jerry. What is wrong with this picture? | Real sleepwalking happens in deep sleep, not while dreaming, and dreaming switches muscles off | Real sleepwalkers can walk on washing lines safely | Cats never sleep | Sleepwalkers always keep their eyes shut
test: What could a family do in the last hour before bed to help everyone sleep? | Turn off bright screens and read a paper book in dim light | Play an exciting video game together to tire everyone out | Turn on all the lights | Drink lots of fizzy cola
test: What would happen if a grown-up's brain did not switch off his muscles in REM sleep? | He might kick, punch or jump out of bed acting out his dreams | He would never dream again, because moving muscles stop all dreams | He would sleep for a week | He would become a sleepwalker in deep sleep only
test: «why do i keep yawning, I'm not even tired, I just read about yawning» What is true? | Yawns are catching, and even reading about them can start one | Yawning only ever happens when you are very, very tired | You are short of food | Yawning means you are ill
test: «i'm gonna beat the no-sleep record, 12 days easy» What would a doctor say? | No, going without sleep is dangerous and record books stopped accepting it | Fine, sleep is not really needed at all, it is just a habit people have got into | Fine, if you drink cola | Good, it makes the brain stronger
exam: State the approximate duration of a single cycle of sleep in a human being. | Ninety minutes | Ten minutes | Four hours | Twelve hours
exam: Identify the stage of sleep during which the majority of vivid dreams are experienced. | The stage of rapid eye movement | The stage of light sleep at the onset of the night | The state of full wakefulness | The stage of deep sleep in the earliest hour
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the awakening of a person who is sleepwalking. | It is not dangerous, merely confusing, and gentle guidance to bed is preferable | It is invariably fatal to the sleepwalker | It causes permanent loss of memory | It is required by law that the sleepwalker be left undisturbed
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the effect of bright screens upon sleep? | They maintain alertness and may delay the secretion of melatonin | They increase the duration of deep sleep | They hasten the onset of sleep in all children | They exert no influence whatsoever
